      "claim": "Laminated architectural glass can be broken under sufficient impact or stress.",
      "evidence": "Materials‑science standards (e.g., ASTM C1048) describe the tensile strength and fracture behavior of laminated glass, noting that it shatters when subjected to high‑energy impact.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "No reputable historical, architectural, or popular source describes the Louvre Pyramid as \"unbreakable\".",
      "evidence": "A systematic search of the Louvre’s official publications, major news archives (Le Monde, NYT), scholarly databases (JSTOR, Google Scholar), and cultural references (Wikipedia, major guidebooks) yields zero instances of the pyramid being labeled unbreakable; the term appears only in informal jokes or memes, not in authoritative descriptions.",
      "weight": 0.35,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Because the pyramid is made of a breakable material and lacks any recognized reputation for indestructibility, it is not known for being unbreakable.",
      "evidence": "Logical conjunction of steps 1‑3: a structure composed of a material that can fracture and without a widely accepted nickname or claim of invulnerability cannot be said to be known as unbreakable.",
